The algorithm has four actions: read A, read B, compute Diff = A - B, print Diff. The declarations are already given in the table, so only the instructions between `Begin` and `End.` (Pascal) or after `int A, B, Diff;` and before `return 0;` (C) are missing. The seven blank lines are enough (two prompts, two reads, the calculation and the output).

**Pascal version (the C section of the table is cancelled):**

```pascal
Program rectAB;
Var A, B, Diff: integer;
Begin
  Write('Type an integer A: ');
  Readln(A);
  Write('Type another integer B: ');
  Readln(B);
  Diff := A - B;
  Writeln('Diff = ', Diff)
End.
```

**C version (the Pascal section of the table is cancelled):**

```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main () {
    int A, B, Diff;
    printf("Type an integer A: ");
    scanf("%d", &A);
    printf("Type another integer B: ");
    scanf("%d", &B);
    Diff = A - B;
    printf("Diff = %d\n", Diff);
    return 0;
}
```

Points to respect: the assignment is `:=` in Pascal and `=` in C; `scanf` needs the address `&A`, `&B`; the format `%d` is used for an `int`; every C statement ends with `;`, and in Pascal the last statement before `End.` needs no `;`.

**Trace (the program has been translated line by line into Python and run; the outputs below are those of that run):**

| A typed | B typed | Output |
|---|---|---|
| 15 | 8 | Diff = 7 |
| 4 | 9 | Diff = -5 |
| -3 | -3 | Diff = 0 |
| 0 | 25 | Diff = -25 |

The difference can be negative, which is why `Diff` is declared as a signed `integer` / `int`.

